ainz, synthesized several existing technologies into a cohesive system around 1440-1450. His crucial innovations included:\n\n**Movable type**: Individual letters cast from a lead-tin alloy that could be arranged, inked, and reused. This drew on Chinese and Korean precedents in typecasting but adapted them to the demands of Latin script and the new press technology.\n\n**The oil-based ink**: Traditional water-based inks would not adhere properly to metal type. Gutenberg developed an oil-based ink that produced clearer impressions and durable text.\n\n**The printing press**: Adapted from the screw presses used in wine and olive oil production, the wooden press applied even pressure across the page, enabling consistent, high-quality impressions.\n\nThe first major product of this technology was the Gutenberg Bible"}